# Data exposure

## Errors and logs

- Production responses to untrusted clients omit stack traces, query text, and
  internal hostnames
- Logs may include an identifier and a failure class. They omit secrets,
  session tokens, and full authentication headers
- Turn debug and admin diagnostics off for production defaults

## Isolation

- In a multi-tenant product, every read and write is scoped by tenant or
  owner. A missing filter is a data leak
- Cache keys include the tenant or the owner. Do not reuse a shared cache
  entry for another tenant's payload
- Authorization and tenant checks run before the data is fetched when the
  fetch itself would cross the boundary

## Sensitive fields

- Do not put identifiers that are secrets, or personal data that is not
  required, into URLs, referrers, or client analytics
- Collect the fields the feature needs. Do not add a new store of personal
  data as a side effect of logging
- Rate-limit authentication and other expensive public endpoints when adding
  them. A new unlimited login route is a credential-stuffing target
